OS X SAT SMART Driver
#####################

This is a kernel driver for Mac OS X external USB or FireWire drives.
It extends the standard driver behaviour by providing access to drive
SMART data. The interface to SMART data is same as with ATA family
driver, so most existing applications should work. 

The driver requires a SAT (SCSI ATA Translation) capable external
drive enclosure. Check smartmontools list for supported devices:
http://smartmontools.org/wiki/Supported_USB-Devices 
If the Option column contains sat or jmicron, the driver should work.
The driver should work with Snow Leopard and Lion and Mountain Lion. 
People have reported problems with Lion and Encrypted volumes. 
Some enclosures are reported to work with FireWire but not with USB.
The driver is not compatible to WD Drive Manager, or enclosures 
with custom kernel extensions.

Note for Yosemite (Mac OS X 10.10) users:
Try first version 0.8. If it works for you, then just use it.
If not or you want to try newer version or participate on development, 
then you must allow unsigned kernel extensions with command:
  sudo nvram boot-args="kext-dev-mode=1"

The code is based on Apple opensource files and is therefore published
under Apple Public Source License. For details see
http://www.opensource.apple.com/license/apsl/

Install
=======
 
 * Unmount external drives
 * Use the dmg image and the installer
 * Check DiskUtility. The disks should have "S.M.A.R.T. Status: Verified"


Uninstall
=========

 * Remove driver and plugin
    sudo rm -r /Library/Extensions/SATSMARTDriver.kext
    sudo rm -r /Library/Extensions/SATSMARTLib.plugin
 * Reboot


Adding enclosure identification
===============================

Check, if the enclosure is identified using shell:

ioreg -r -w 0 -c fi_dungeon_driver_IOSATDriver  | egrep 'Enclosure|PassThroughMode|Capable'|grep -v Identifiers
  |   "Enclosure Name" = "Unknown 0928:000a"
  |   "SATSMARTCapable" = Yes
  |   "PassThroughMode" = "sat16"

Add an issue to GitHub.

Compile
=======

 * Compile all targets
    make package
    Or, xcodebuild (use -sdk macosx10.9 on Mac OS X 10.9 to force the use of the platform SDK -
    it may be wise to do the equivalent on other OS versions).

 * Unmount all existing external drives.

 * Load the kernel extension.
    sudo tail -f /private/var/log/kernel.log &
    sudo make install

 * The external drives should mount automatically.

 * Test
    cd SATSMARTDriver/build/Debug/
    ./smart_status
    ./smart_sample -a

 * Check DiskUtility. The disk should have "S.M.A.R.T. Status: Verified"

 * Install the driver to system permanently
    sudo make realinstall

 * Reboot

 * If you want to limit the driver for certain hardware add product 
   and vendor identification to SATSMARTDriver/Info.plist.
   Check chapter "Adding enclosure identification"


Troubleshooting
===============

SMART Query returns "kIOReturnUnsupported"
------------------------------------------

This error occurs when the driver cannot communicate with the device
using SAT (SCSI-ATA Translation) protocol. Common causes:

1. Apple Silicon Internal NVMe (disk0)
   Apple's internal NVMe drives use a proprietary controller
   (AppleANS3CGv2Controller) that does not support SAT protocol.
   This driver CANNOT support Apple Silicon internal drives.
   
   Alternative tools for Apple NVMe health monitoring:
   * Install smartmontools: brew install smartmontools
   * Query health: sudo smartctl -a /dev/disk0
   * Basic status: system_profiler SPNVMeDataType

2. Third-Party Driver Conflict (e.g., Samsung Magician)
   Some manufacturers install their own kernel extensions that
   intercept SMART queries. The SAT driver cannot take over while
   these are loaded.
   
   To resolve:
   * Unmount the affected drive: diskutil unmountDisk diskN
   * Unload the conflicting driver:
     sudo kmutil unload -b com.samsung.magicianpssd.driver
   * Re-run ./smart_status to verify
   
   For permanent resolution, uninstall the manufacturer's software
   (e.g., Samsung Magician) or remove its kexts from /Library/Extensions/

3. Virtual Devices (Disk Images, SD Cards)
   Disk images and SD cards do not support SMART.
   The smart_status tool now reports "N/A" for these devices.

4. Unsupported USB/FireWire Enclosure
   Not all enclosures support SAT passthrough. Check:
   http://smartmontools.org/wiki/Supported_USB-Devices
   
   Look for "sat" or "jmicron" in the Option column.


Checking Loaded Kernel Extensions
---------------------------------

To see which storage-related kexts are loaded:
  kmutil showloaded | grep -iE '(samsung|smart|sat)'

To check system logs for driver messages:
  log show --predicate 'eventMessage contains "SMART"' --last 5m


Building on Modern macOS
------------------------

The Xcode project builds with current Xcode for macOS 12 and later:

  make clean build pkg CONFIGURATION=Release

This produces a universal package. The kext is built for arm64e (the
kernel ABI on Apple Silicon; plain arm64 kexts do not load) and x86_64,
the plugin and tools for arm64 and x86_64. Everything installs to
/Library/Extensions and /usr/local/bin; /System is read-only since
macOS 10.15 and is never touched.

Loading a third-party kext on macOS 11 and later needs, once:
 * on Apple Silicon, "Reduced Security" with "Allow user management of
   kernel extensions from identified developers" set in recoveryOS
   Startup Security Utility;
 * approval in System Settings > Privacy & Security after installing;
 * a restart.


Signing and Notarization
------------------------

Unsigned builds work only with kext signature checks relaxed. For a
package that installs without Gatekeeper warnings, pass Developer ID
identities:

  make clean build pkg notarize CONFIGURATION=Release \
      SIGN_APP="Developer ID Application: Name (TEAMID)" \
      SIGN_INSTALLER="Developer ID Installer: Name (TEAMID)"

Notarization uses the Apple developer account signed into Xcode
(Settings > Accounts); no notarytool profile or app-specific password
is needed. notarize-pkg.sh uploads the package inside a disposable app
archive, waits for Apple, then staples the ticket to the package.
For the kext to load, the Developer ID Application certificate must
also carry Apple's kext signing extension, which Apple grants on
request: https://developer.apple.com/contact/kext/
